WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.343
The exchange may take fifteen minutes, but ordinary HTTP
connections end after thirty seconds. This local dashboard

00:00:07.343 --> 00:00:10.919
separates acceptance, background work, and live updates.

00:00:16.108 --> 00:00:23.855
Choose a delay and a reproducible seed. The request returns
accepted promptly. A separate Python worker waits locally, while

00:00:23.855 --> 00:00:26.041
the saved ledger remains available.

00:00:30.642 --> 00:00:38.664
This pull really waits thirty two seconds. The acceptance request
has already ended. During the wait, the ledger stays usable. The

00:00:38.664 --> 00:00:46.687
worker commits all records and its completion event atomically. A
blocking pipe wakes one ordered event dispatcher. WebSocket then

00:00:46.687 --> 00:00:54.586
updates both tabs without polling. Moving a remote HTTP request
into a worker would not bypass an upstream network timeout; this

00:00:54.586 --> 00:00:56.561
mock processing happens locally.

00:01:04.208 --> 00:01:11.930
Repeating the same seed skips duplicates. Search and pagination
stay in place during automatic updates. Durable events replay

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:16.439
after a disconnection, and database transactions prevent partial
batches.

00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:28.570
The code separates API orchestration, the worker, the seeded
provider, and SQLite transactions. Reproducible tests cover

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:36.830
timeouts, concurrent requests, failure rollback, ordered delivery,
and restart recovery. The nine hundred second setting is validated

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:41.115
separately; this recording does not claim a full fifteen minute
wait.
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.120
Current dashboard and actual captured HTTP 202 response.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:55.120
The running job is separate from the completed request.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.817
The full 32-second completion was shown earlier.
